From 5a15f7450d090587c45132254015cbb009a96e54 Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: yekai pan <pyk@seas.upenn.edu>
Date: Sun, 25 Oct 2026 07:38:16 -0400
Subject: [PATCH 08/24] l2r-local: entry 21: cancel rcgmp retains of [value]
 record members against the record's drop

A by-value record (or tuple) taken apart retains each member it extracts
and then drops the record:

  %a = reussir.record.extract(%t) [0] ; rcgmp.retain %a
  ...
  reussir.ref.drop(reussir.ref.spilled(%t))

The first acquire/drop expansion turns the drop into one release per
member, each read back through the record's spill:

  %s = reussir.ref.spilled(%t)
  rcgmp.release (reussir.ref.load (reussir.ref.project %s [0]))
  ...

With rc members the same shape cancels completely in the second inc/dec
cancellation run: EquivalenceAnalysis knows that `record.extract %t[i]`
and the load at [i] through `ref.spilled %t` are the same value (its rule
for rc pointers, `valueRecordField`). With integer members (`!rcgmp.mpz`)
every retain and every member release stayed, for two reasons:

- cancelCountedWordRetains (#801) scans from the retain only across
  retains and operations that are pure or only read memory
  (`leavesCountsAlone`). `ref.spilled` allocates its stack slot and writes
  the value into it, so the scan stopped there, before every member
  release.
- EquivalenceAnalysis applied the extract/spill rule to rc pointers only.
  Values of other types were equivalent only when both were loads, so the
  retained extract and the released load were never MustAlias.

So a loop that builds a [value] record or a tuple of Nats and takes it
apart in every step kept a retain and a release per member and step, each
with its immediate test: 20 tag branches in the loop of a four-member
record, where the same loop with rc members has none.

The change:

- EquivalenceAnalysis::decideLoadedEquivalent (values of the types other
  than ref, rc and nullable) also accepts the same field of the same
  by-value record read by extract on one side and through the record's
  spill on the other, the rule rc pointers already have.
- leavesCountsAlone also accepts an operation whose only effects other
  than reads are an Allocate and a Write on its own result: it changes no
  count and no stored value. That is `ref.spilled`, which the record's
  drop releases its members through, but the scan now also crosses the
  other operations of that kind: `reussir.token.alloc`,
  `reussir.region.create`, `rcgmp.extsi`/`extui`/`fptosi`,
  `rcgmp.bridge.mpz.promote`, `memref.alloc`/`alloca`.
- leavesCountsAlone rejects `rcgmp.bridge.mpz.is_unique`, which it
  accepted before as a read: it reads the count that the retain makes
  shared, so the scan stops there, as the rc scan stops at `rc.is_unique`.
  No pipeline reaches it today (the op appears only in the
  `__reussir_rcgmp_ffi_is_unique` glue, inside an `scf.if`); the stop is
  defensive.

The other rules stay: the scan stops at the first release of any word, at
calls, at operations with regions and at every operation that writes or
frees other memory (an operation with a fresh result that consumes its
operands, such as `rcgmp.addi`, or `reussir.token.free`), and the release
must be MustAlias the retained word. As for rc members, a member
extracted out of field order keeps its pair (the scan meets another
member's release first). The equivalence change only reaches the alias
queries of the counted-word cancellation: the other users of the analysis
ask about refs, rc pointers and nullables, or compare loads with loads.

Effect: the four-member loop (and its tuple twin) loses its 4 member
retains and 4 member releases (asm at -O aggressive 384 to 295 lines, 36
to 22 test-and-branch instructions), on this base (l2r-stepd, with the
drop patches) as on l2r-base7: the first acquire/drop expansion releases a
by-value record's integer members through its spill here too. lean2rr dev
2c4083d9 under --int-backend rcgmp, the same .rr compiled with and without
this patch (valgrind builds, instruction counts; the native outputs
identical): L12 (sieve, 5*10^7) 11.739G to 10.617G (-9.6%), L17 17.544G to
16.170G (-7.8%), Z01c 22.910G to 21.525G (-6.0%).

Tests: conversion/inc_dec_cancellation_counted_word_value_record.mlir (a
four-member record, a tuple, a record with a trivial member: nothing left;
one extracted member: only the other member's release; a member used
twice: one retain left; an `rcgmp.extui` between the retain and the drop
is crossed; the pair stays when an `rcgmp.addi` of other words or a
`reussir.token.free` sits between them, when the member's count is read by
`rcgmp.bridge.mpz.is_unique`, when the member escapes into a call, the
record escapes into a call, the member is consumed while the record is
read again, the dropped record is another one, or the release is another
field's), frontend/bigint_value_record_members.rr (+ .c: the loop with a
record and with a tuple, a member used twice, a record still used after
the extract, a record with Nat, u64 and Int members; each test takes its
seed from the C driver (1 and 2, both branches), so the heap and immediate
integers are computed and the records chosen at run time and nothing folds
to a constant; run at the default level and at -O aggressive) and its ASan
twin sanitizer/bigint-value-record-members-asan.rr (counts balanced: no
use after free, no leak).
